Title:
ExecuteAssemblyPICO

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
ExecuteAssemblyPICO is a Position Independent Code (PIC) implementation of an “execute-assembly” capability for running .NET assemblies from a native loader. It focuses on packaging a .NET assembly runner as PIC using the Crystal Palace linker, enabling in-memory style execution workflows without a traditional PE loader footprint. The loader redirects the current process stdout to a named pipe to capture and relay the managed assembly’s output. It then loads the PICO runner and calls an exported `ExecuteAssembly` function, passing the target assembly bytes/path and arguments. This is primarily useful for red teams and penetration testers who want a modular execute-assembly primitive for post-exploitation (e.g., running tools like Rubeus). It’s interesting because it combines PIC tradecraft with managed execution and output capture, aligning with common C2 “execute-assembly” patterns.

Technical Focus:
- Position Independent Code (PIC) payload construction
- Crystal Palace linker usage for PICO generation
- .NET assembly execution via a native loader/runner
- Named pipe stdout redirection/output capture
- Exported function interface (`ExecuteAssembly`) for invocation

Use Cases:
- Execute .NET post-exploitation tooling (e.g., Rubeus) from a native implant/loader
- Integrate an “execute-assembly” module into custom C2/agent frameworks
- Capture managed tool output reliably via named pipes instead of console inheritance
- Build PIC-based loaders to reduce reliance on standard PE loading patterns

Keywords:
execute-assembly, PIC, Position Independent Code, PICO, Crystal Palace linker, .NET assembly, CLR hosting, in-memory execution, native loader, named pipe, stdout redirection, ExecuteAssembly export, post-exploitation, Rubeus, tradecraftgarden, inject-assembly, Windows, red team tooling